  "worked_solution": "From the identity $1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta $ with $\\theta =x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n1-{sin}^{2}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})={cos}^{2}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}|\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
